Severity reflects reachable source, control bypass, sink, blast radius, and required privileges at this repository revision. Critical means a low-privilege tenant can cross into the host or other tenants with high confidentiality and integrity impact. High means a remote path can expose credentials or private data, bypass authentication or ownership, reach host-only services, or exhaust the public service. Medium means the issue requires an existing session, operator action, or upstream compromise, or has a narrower lifecycle or availability impact.

CVSS 4.0 environmental scores are not assigned because deployment topology, GitHub App permissions, Datadog retention, host credentials, and reverse-proxy limits were unavailable. The severity rationales identify those environmental dependencies explicitly.

## Scope and Method

The audit covered 216 tracked files and approximately 55 HTTP or WebSocket routes. It traced browser, API, OAuth, GitHub App, control database, tenant database, Git, workspace filesystem, sidecar, Podman, terminal, model-provider, runner, telemetry, backup, deletion, and dependency boundaries.

Methods included:

- route-by-route authentication, authorization, ownership, CSRF, and tenant-store review;
- source-to-sink tracing for filesystem paths, Git URLs, subprocesses, SQL, HTTP clients, sidecar messages, WebSockets, and rendered content;
- disposable local probes using temporary directories, synthetic users, inert credentials, mocked upstreams, and loopback listeners;
- Semgrep, Bandit, `pip-audit`, both npm audits, OSV-Scanner, gitleaks history scanning, Trivy, and Hadolint;
- full backend, frontend, build, and sidecar validation;
- manual reachability review of every scanner alert before assigning finding status.

No probe contacted GitHub, Datadog, a model provider, production, or another third party. No application code, configuration, database, infrastructure, or GitHub setting was changed.

